E. Root Cause Analysis

  Profile              Candidate   Top 10    Constructed  Score      Strong Cats /  Verdict
                       Pool Size   Avg Score Avg Score    Drop       Weak Cats
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Retirement Planner   1,019        59.7       39.2       20.5 pts   0 / 27       🔴 Weak construction
  Capital Preservation   544        78.0       65.3       12.7 pts  14 /  4       🔴 Mixed (construction
                                                                                  drops but pool is good)
  Aggressive Growth    1,697        52.8       51.7        1.0 pts   0 / 45       🔴 Weak candidate pools

  Retirement Planner:
    - Strong candidates exist (HY-AR funds score 57-63, DT-USD scores 60)
    - But constructor picks EQ-LC (score 10.5) and EQ-MLC (score 24.6) as "Core"
      instead of high-scoring HY-AR / DT funds
    - 20.5-point drop indicates construction is prioritizing strategy group coverage
      (needs large cap, flexi cap, etc.) over score quality
    - AMC cap=1 prevents picking multiple top HY-AR funds from same AMC

  Capital Preservation:
    - Strong candidate pool (DT categories average 50-65, all rated Strong/Adequate)
    - Construction achieves 65.3 which is respectable, but top 10 eligible average 78.0
    - 12.7-point drop because constructor diversifies across DT subtypes (CB, GL, SD, LD)
      rather than picking the absolute highest-scoring funds
    - Still the best-performing profile due to strong DT candidate pool

  Aggressive Growth:
    - ALL 45 eligible categories are Very Weak (avg scores 8-35)
    - No category has more than 2 funds scoring over 50 in the entire pool
    - Construction achieves 51.7 — essentially the best the pool can offer (52.8 top 10 avg)
    - Only 1.0-point drop — constructor is picking the best available
    - Root cause: equity categories have poor scoreV3 scores due to:
      a) High volatility penalized heavily in profile-adaptive scoring
      b) Category-relative scoring means top equity funds still score low
         when compared against low-volatility DT benchmarks
      c) scoreV3's conservative-leaning weights penalize aggressive categories
         (volatility penalty, consistency requirements)

  VERDICT SUMMARY:
    The scoring engine (scoreV3) is designed with a conservative bias —
    debt categories naturally score higher due to low volatility, high
    Sharpe/Sortino, and consistency. Equity categories score lower because:
    - Higher volatility triggers penalties
    - Category-relative scoring within equity categories is harsh
      (all equity funds are volatile relative to debt benchmarks)
    - Profile-adaptive weights for aggressive still favor Sortino over CAGR

    This means:
    - Capital Preservation works well (strong DT pools + high DT scores)
    - Retirement Planner suffers from construction forcing equity allocation
      (the allocation model mandates equity categories, but those score poorly)
    - Aggressive Growth appears weak but is actually operating at the ceiling
      of what the candidate pool can provide

    RECOMMENDATION: The scoring formula itself needs recalibration for aggressive
    profiles. ScoreV3 weights aggressively toward Sortino and consistency, which
    inherently disadvantages equity funds. Consider adjusting profile weights so
    that aggressive profiles reward CAGR more and penalize volatility less.

